{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>A journey of three days.</b> As far as the land of Israel — for indeed in three journeys they reached the area facing Kadesh-barnea in the wilderness of Paran, from which they sent the spies, as Moses our master explained in saying: וַתִּקְרְבוּן אֵלַי כֻּלְּכֶם וַתֹּאמְרוּ נִשְׁלְחָה אֲנָשִׁים (Deuteronomy 1:22). This is the place of their encampment in the wilderness of Paran that he mentioned here in saying: וְאַחַר נָסְעוּ הָעָם מֵחֲצֵרוֹת וגו׳ (Numbers 12:16), where the spies came upon their return, as it says: וַיָּשֻׁבוּ וַיָּבֹאוּ אֶל מִדְבַּר פָּארָן קָדֵשָׁה (Numbers 13:26). This is the place they called Rithmah, as it says: וַיִּסְעוּ מֵחֲצֵרוֹת וַיַּחֲנוּ בְּרִתְמָה (Numbers 33:18), and there he listed three journeys from the wilderness of Sinai to that place — Kibroth-hattaavah, Hazeroth, and Rithmah — for the incident of Taberah occurred on the way, not at a time of encampment." "<b>And the ark of the covenant of HaShem journeyed before them a journey of three days.</b> During those three days when they traveled through those three journeys in the great and terrible wilderness (Deuteronomy 1:19), the ark journeyed before them to secure the way from serpents and scorpions and the like. But during the rest of the journeys the ark traveled within the camps like the rest of the Kohathite burden (Numbers 2:17)." "<b>To seek out for them a resting-place.</b> A secure encampment in the terrible wilderness."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "Sforno argues that the 'three days' journey' brought Israel to the threshold of the land (Kadesh-barnea), identifying the three stages by cross-referencing Numbers 33 and Deuteronomy 1."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:39:21.719283008Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}